WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.400
What are AI agent skills and&nbsp;
why have they become an open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.040
standard adopted by practically&nbsp;
every major AI coding platform?

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:14.880
Well, because skills address a&nbsp;
specific problem with agents.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.760
Now AI agents, they're pretty good reasoners and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.880
LLMs or large language models&nbsp;
already know a lot of facts.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.000
They can tell you about Kubernetes&nbsp;
architecture or the the history of SQL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.880
or the airspeed velocity of an unladen swallow,&nbsp;
that part is covered but they lack something.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.280
They lack procedural knowledge.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.120
The stuff that's specific to&nbsp;
how work actually gets done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:57.200
like let's say a 47-step workflow for&nbsp;
generating a compliant financial report.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.920
Yeah, that would be fun.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.240
An AI agent that is running a&nbsp;
large language model when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.880
encounters a task like generating this&nbsp;
report, it basically has two options.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:20.000
Either somebody needs to prompt it with every&nbsp;
single step, all 47 of them, and they need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.720
to do that every time, or worse still, the&nbsp;
agent is just going to take a guess at it.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:34.720
Now a skill is how you actually&nbsp;
add in that procedural knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:41.840
into the agent and the format of a&nbsp;
skill is almost comically simple.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.880
It's simply a skill.md file.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.480
That's a markdown file in a folder.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.200
So let me draw out what a&nbsp;
skill actually looks like.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.120
So at the top.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:04.240
Skill.md file is some YAML front letter.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.920
So let's have a look at what&nbsp;
is defined in the front letter?

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.360
Well, at a minimum, there are two things.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:21.680
So there is a name and there is description.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.720
These are the two mandatory fields.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.480
Now the name identifies the skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:35.120
the description that tells the agent what&nbsp;
this skill does and when it should be used.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:42.400
Now this description is pretty important&nbsp;
because it's the trigger condition that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:50.080
tells agent exactly when this skill applies,&nbsp;
so maybe the skill name is PDF Builder.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:57.120
And the description here that says something like,&nbsp;
use this when the user asks to extract a PDF.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.160
Now there are some other fields you can put&nbsp;
into the front matter like author and version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.200
but it's name and description that are mandatory.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.840
Now below the front mater, we also have a field.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.240
Now these are the actual instructions.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.560
These are the step-by-step workflows, the&nbsp;
rules, the examples of input and output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.440
whatever the agent needs to know to do the job.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.040
And it's just written in plain markdown.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.640
And then the skill folder can contain&nbsp;
some optional folders as well.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.240
So you don't have to have&nbsp;
these, but you can add them.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:41.680
One of those optional folders is the scripts.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:51.120
And this has executable JavaScript or Python&nbsp;
or bash that the agent can actually run.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:58.480
There's also a references directory that&nbsp;
contains additional documentation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.240
gets loaded if the agent determines it needs it.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.680
And finally, the other optional&nbsp;
directory is the assets directory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.440
that contains static resources&nbsp;
like templates and data files.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:20.640
That's what an agent skill looks like, but&nbsp;
agents can have lots of skills defined for them.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.680
So what happens when there are&nbsp;
like hundreds of these skills?

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.840
Loading all of them into the LLM&nbsp;
context window at startup would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.640
blow through the token budget before&nbsp;
anyone even gets to ask a question.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:41.840
So skills use something&nbsp;
called progressive disclosure.

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.080
And progressive disclosure works in three tiers.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.080
So tier one is metadata only, and that applies.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.720
So at startup, the agent loads just the&nbsp;
name and description from each skill.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.760
So that's just a handful of tokens per skill.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:07.760
So even if there are a hundred skills installed,&nbsp;
the overhead isn't gonna fill the context window.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.240
And this is kind of essentially&nbsp;
akin to a skills table of contents.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.440
Now tier two, this relates&nbsp;
to the full instructions.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.520
When the agent sees a request that&nbsp;
matches this skill's description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.640
it reads the complete skill.md body into context.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.600
And this tells the agent what to&nbsp;
do, the skill we are teaching it.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.960
And that identification, the matching&nbsp;
of a given requirement for a task to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.600
the skill available is something that&nbsp;
happens through the LLM's own reasoning.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.280
The model decides when it&nbsp;
can make use of the skill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.320
which is why a good skill&nbsp;
description is so important.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.480
Then tier three, that's&nbsp;
these optional folders here.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:00.240
So these are the resources that maps to the&nbsp;
scripts and references and the assets folders.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.400
And they only get loaded when a&nbsp;
specific task actually needs them.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.040
So the agent starts with a lightweight&nbsp;
index of everything it can do.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.120
That's the name and description.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.760
It pulls in the detailed instructions&nbsp;
when they're relevant, the body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.160
based on matching the trigger condition.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.920
And it grabs resources only at the point of need.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.240
Now skills bring a type of knowledge to agents.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.720
There are several ways to&nbsp;
incorporate knowledge into an agent.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:34.560
So let's just briefly compare them&nbsp;
because they handle different things.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.120
And the first one I just want to mention is MCP.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.240
That's Model Context Protocol.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.280
What sort of knowledge does MCP give you?

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.200
Gives you tool access.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:53.440
It gives agents the ability to call out to&nbsp;
external APIs and to interact with services.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.120
MCP is about what the agent can reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:03.120
but it doesn't tell the agent when to&nbsp;
reach for it or what to do once it has.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.720
So that's MCP.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:10.240
Another one is RAG, Retrieval&nbsp;
Augmented Generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.120
and RAG that handles factual&nbsp;
knowledge, so it pulls in...

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.000
Relevant chunks from our&nbsp;
knowledge database at run time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.000
which is pretty handy when the&nbsp;
agent needs to look something up.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.960
But RAG doesn't teach an&nbsp;
agent how to do something.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.520
It's reference material.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.720
What about another one?

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.240
How about fine tuning?

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.680
What can that do for us?

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:40.560
Well, fine tuning bakes knowledge&nbsp;
directly into the model's weights.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.040
Now that's something that's&nbsp;
permanent, but it's expensive.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.880
And if the model changes the&nbsp;
fine-tuning has to be redone.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:58.080
Now skills don't really do any of this so&nbsp;
what knowledge do skills bring to agents?

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:05.440
Well skills handle as I mentioned&nbsp;
right up front procedural knowledge.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.800
It's how to do things in what&nbsp;
order and with what judgment,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.800
and because they just files Well,&nbsp;
they can be version control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.360
they could be easily updated and you&nbsp;
can easily move them between platforms.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.080
Now, in practice, skills will&nbsp;
often use some of these other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.680
forms of knowledge like, well, MCP for example.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.840
So MCP provides the capability to invoke something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.680
externally and the skill provides the&nbsp;
judgment for when and how to do that.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.200
Now, one more thing to say about skills.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.840
Is that the skill.md format is an open standard,

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:56.880
and it's published at agent skills.io&nbsp;
and that's an Apache 2.0 license project,

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.480
and it was adopted across a bunch of AI platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.040
like Claude Code and OpenAI&nbsp;
Codex and many other tools.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:11.280
So a skill built for one platform works&nbsp;
on any platform that supports this spec.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.160
Now there's a useful way to think about&nbsp;
skills and it comes from cognitive science.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:19.520
Now humans have distinct types of memory.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:22.720
There's semantic memory, which are facts.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.840
So Rome is the capital of Italy.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:29.920
There's episodic memory, which are experiences.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.040
So, uh, I went to Rome last summer.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.200
Actually I did, and it was lovely.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:41.520
Uh, and then there's procedural memory, which&nbsp;
are skills like how to ride a scooter on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:47.200
streets of Rome and live to tell&nbsp;
the tale, which I also did barely.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:52.640
Now agent architectures are&nbsp;
starting to mirror this.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:59.120
So semantic memory, that maps&nbsp;
pretty closely to retrieval,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:02.400
augmented generation and knowledge bases.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:03.680
Episodic memory.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.080
Well, that really maps to conversational.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.640
Logs and interaction history&nbsp;
and procedural memory.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:20.480
Well, yep, that maps quite nicely to skill files.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:29.280
Now, one thing that does need mentioning&nbsp;
is that skills can include executable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:36.320
scripts with access to file systems&nbsp;
and environment variables and API keys.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:45.920
That's what makes them powerful, but&nbsp;
it's also what makes trust so important.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:50.080
Because when an agent runs one of these scripts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.760
it's typically executing&nbsp;
commands locally on your machine

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.560
and audits have found publicly&nbsp;
available skills frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:04.720
contain bad stuff like prompt injection, bad stuff

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:10.240
like tool poisoning, bad&nbsp;
stuff like hidden malware.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:13.840
Basically the usual suspects&nbsp;
for any open ecosystem.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:18.320
So, so treat skill installation&nbsp;
the way that any responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.600
team treats installing any software dependency,

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:28.480
which is to say, review it and understand what&nbsp;
it does before using it on your local machine.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:30.560
So, So where does this leave us?

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.640
Well, skills are procedural memory for AI agents.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.480
They're defined in a markdown&nbsp;
file that lives in a folder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:42.960
that teaches an agent how to do a specific job.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:48.080
Skills are conditionally triggered&nbsp;
and they load efficiently through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:52.480
progressive disclosure and the&nbsp;
format is an open standard.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:56.240
So an agent that already&nbsp;
knows the airspeed velocity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.600
of an unlaid and swallow, African and European,

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:05.120
can now also learn how to perform any&nbsp;
repeatable task you define for it.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.360
So that's AI agent skills.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:15.680
If you're using them, let me know in the comments.

